registerRefData('nsw-cond-n-307',{"id":307,"pan":null,"ref":"DA-2024/286","address":"465 Princes Highway Rockdale 2216,469 Princes Highway Rockdale 2216,5-7 Geeves Avenue Rockdale 2216","description":"Co-Living Rockdale","outcome":"approved","outcome_raw":"Approved","outcome_source":"planning_panels","determined":"2025-07-08","notice_url":"https://apps.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/prweb/PRRestService/DocMgmt/v1/PublicDocuments/DATA-WORKATTACH-FILE PEC-DPE-EP-WORK PPSSEC-341!20260122T221537.976 GMT","sha256":"2e4fd20e04090b7cf0db40ed644589493398610d26e3ef6b77b7a9d56721c840","pages":95,"layout":"numbered","n":28,"fetched_at":"2026-08-16T23:19:06+00:00","file":"n/307.js","source_page":"https://www.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/planning-panel/co-living-rockdale","lga":"BAYSIDE","conditions":[{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"Legislation Cited: Environmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979","text":"(NSW), ss 4.15, 8.7, 8.15\nLand and Environment Court Act 1979 (NSW), s 34\nWater Management Act 2000 (NSW), s 90\nBayside Local Environmental Plan 2011, cll 2.7, 4.3,\n4.6, 6.3, 6.7, 6.10, 6.11\nEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Regulation\n2021, s 38\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Housing) 2021,\nCh 3 Pt 3, ss 67, 69\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Resilience and\nHazards) 2021, Ch 4, s 4.6\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Transport and\nInfrastructure) 2021, ss 2.48, 2.99, 2.100, 2.119, 2.120\nCases Cited: McMillan v Taylor (2023) 111 NSWLR 634; [2023]\nNSWCA 183\nTexts Cited: Bayside Development Control Plan 2022\nCategory: Principal judgment\nParties: Emag Apartments Pty Ltd (Applicant)\nBayside Council (Respondent)\nRepresentation Counsel:\nA Pickles SC (Applicant)\nP Brown (Solicitor) (Respondent)\nSolicitors:\nMills Oakley (Applicant)\nHWL Ebsworth (Respondent)\nFile Number(s): 2025/94872\nPublication Restriction: Nil","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":1},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"JUDGMENT","text":"1 COMMISSIONER: These proceedings, brought under Class 1 of the Court's\njurisdiction, are an appeal pursuant to s 8.7 of the Environmental Planning and\nAssessment Act 1979 (NSW) (EPA Act) against the deemed refusal of\nDevelopment Application DA-2024/286 (DA) by Bayside Council (Council).\n2 The site is at 465-469 Princes Highway and 5-7 Geeves Avenue, Rockdale,\ncomprising: Lot A in DP 315664, Lot A in DP 306355, Lot Bin DP 315664, Lot A\nin DP 402977, Lot 1 in DP 131822 and Lot B in DP 402977.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":2},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"","text":"The DA seeks consent for demolition of the site's existing structures and the\nconstruction of ground commercial floorspace with ten levels of co-living housing\nabove. Basement parking is proposed.\nConciliation and agreement between the parties","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"The Court arranged a conciliation conference between the parties under s 34(1)","text":"of the Land and Environment Court Act 1979 (NSW) (LEC Act), which was held\non 16 December 2025 and at which I presided. The parties had filed an agreement\nas to the terms of a decision in the proceedings that would be acceptable to the\nparties. This decision involved the Court upholding the appeal and granting\nconsent to the DA, as amended, in accordance with agreed conditions.\nJurisdiction","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"","text":"Under s 34(3) of the LEC Act, I must dispose of the proceedings in accordance\nwith the parties' decision if it is a decision that the Court could have made in the\nproper exercise of its functions. The point of consideration here is whether there\nare any jurisdictional constraints to the exercise of the function to grant\ndevelopment consent in accordance with the parties' agreement (McMillan v\nTaylor (2023) 111 NSWLR 634; [2023] NSWCA 183 at [62]-[65]).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"6","code":null,"number":"6","title":"","text":"Ultimately, I find that there are none. But there are certain statutory queries which\nrequire attention before this function can be exercised by the Court. I attend to the\n3 relevant matters below, assisted by the advice in the parties agreed jurisdictional\nstatement (JS), as amended, and received by the Court on 16 December 2025.\nIntegrated development","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":3},{"label":"7","code":null,"number":"7","title":"","text":"The parties advise that, in order for it to be carried out, the proposal also requires\napproval under s 90(2) of the Water Management Act 2000 (NSW). In turn the\nproposal was referred to WaterNSW as integrated development. General terms of\napproval have subsequently been issued by WaterNSW and incorporated into\nagreed consent conditions.\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Housing) 2021","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"8","code":null,"number":"8","title":"","text":"Part 3 of Ch 3 (co-living housing) applies. The proposal is permissible under s\n67(b) given the site zoning of El - Local Centre which, relevantly, permits shop\ntop housing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"9","code":null,"number":"9","title":"Section 69 outlines standards for co-living housing. Section 69(1) establishes","text":"certain development standards. Section 69(2) indicates various matters which\notherwise must be considered by the consent authority. The parties work through\nthese matters relevantly in their jurisdictional statement and advise that the DA,\nas amended, complies with each of the development standards nominated at\ns 69(1). I accept this advice and am satisfied with regard to the matters at s\n69(1)(a)-(h). The parties also advise, in the JS, of their consideration of the matters\nat s 69(2). This is noted.\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Resilience and Hazards) 2021","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"10","code":null,"number":"10","title":"","text":"Chapter 4 (Remediation of Land) applies with s 4.6 providing that a consent\nauthority must not grant consent to a development unless it has considered\nwhether a site is contaminated or potentially contaminated land and, if it is, that it\nis satisfied that the site is suitable (or will be suitable after undergoing remediation)\nfor the proposed use. The parties indicate that a detailed site investigation and\nremediation action plan have been prepared to ensure the site will be suitable for\nthe proposed development. Compliance with the remediation action plan is\n4 required under the agreed consent conditions. On the basis of the parties' advice,\nI am satisfied with respect to s 4.6.\nState Environmental Planning Policy (Transport and Infrastructure) 2021","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"11","code":null,"number":"11","title":"Section 2.48 is triggered as a consequence of overhead power lines running","text":"adjacent to the site along Princes Highway. I am advised that Council gave the\nnecessary notice to Ausgrid, which has nominated certain requirements which\nhave been incorporated into agreed consent conditions.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"12","code":null,"number":"12","title":"","text":"Given the site's proximity to the rail corridor, a referral notice was also issued to\nTransport for NSW (TfNSW), as the relevant rail authority, under s 2.99. The\nTfNSW response raised an issue relating to access along Geeves Avenue, which\nhas been subsequently resolved to the satisfaction of the parties and TfNSW (JS\npar 34). The requirements of s 2.99 have been met.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"13","code":null,"number":"13","title":"Section 2.100(3) and 2.120(3) require findings of satisfaction in relation to","text":"acoustics in consideration of rail and road noise respectively. The parties agree\nthat the noise assessment accompanying the amended DA notes the\nconsideration of relevant guidelines and makes findings in regard to construction\nrecommendations to ensure compliance with the specified standards. Compliance\nwith the acoustic report is required under agreed Condition 1, 30 and 127. I accept\nthis advice and am satisfied with respect to ss 2 .100(3) and 2.119(3).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"14","code":null,"number":"14","title":"Section 2.119 applies to development with frontage to a classified road and","text":"applies given the site's frontage to Princes Highway. It requires certain findings of\nsatisfaction before power is available for the granting of consent. With respect to\ns 2.119(2)(a), I am satisfied that the vehicular access to the site is provided by a\nroad other than the classified road (ie vehicular access is via Geeves Lane). I am\nalso satisfied with respect to s 2.119(2)(b). This is based on the parties' advice,\nrelying on relevant technical evaluation, on the net benefits of the proposal in\nterms of traffic generation compared to the existing setting. I am satisfied with\nrespect to s 2.119(2)(c) given my earlier findings on acoustics and agreed consent\nconditions relating to construction.\n5 Bayside Local Environmental Plan 2011","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"15","code":null,"number":"15","title":"","text":"The parties advise that consideration has been given to the objectives of the\nrelevant El Local Centre zone. The parties indicate their agreement that the\nproposal, as now further amended, is consistent with the objectives. I further note\nthat demolition is permissible pursuant to cl 2.7.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"16","code":null,"number":"16","title":"","text":"The proposal contravenes cl 4.3 which sets a maximum building height of 35m\nand relies on cl 4.6 (relating to exceptions to development standards). In\naccordance with the Environmental Planning and Assessment Regulation 2021\n(NSW), the DA is accompanied by a document setting out the grounds upon which\nthe applicant seeks to demonstrate the requirements of cl 4.6(3) have been met\n(prepared by Planning Ingenuity dated 24 October 2025 (Planning Ingenuity\ndocumentation)). The maximum contravention is 1.87m, comprising the lift\noverrun. The parties agree that, on the basis of the amendments to the DA, and\nreferencing the Planning Ingenuity Documentation arguments, compliance with cl\n4.3 is unreasonable and unnecessary and there are sufficient environmental\nplanning grounds to justify the contravention of the development standard. I agree\nwith the parties and find that the applicant, through the Planning Ingenuity\ndocumentation, has demonstrated that:\n(1) Compliance with the development standard is unreasonable or\nunnecessary in the circumstances. This is through the Planning Ingenuity\ndocumentation (Section 4) working through each of the objectives of cl 4.3\nand demonstrating they are relevantly satisfied. It is my finding that the\napplicant has demonstrated that there is no need for strict compliance with\nthe building height standard because it has demonstrated to my satisfaction\nthat the objectives of the clause have been achieved in any event.\n(2) There are sufficient environmental planning grounds to justify the\ncontravention. The Planning Ingenuity documentation works through a\nseries of convincing points of justification in its Section 5. Of particular note\nfor me is the relative minor scale of the contravention given the fact of the\n6 buildings design qualities and the permissibility of taller buildings in the\nnearby vicinity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"17","code":null,"number":"17","title":"Clause 6.3 concerns stormwater and water sensitive urban design. Prior to the","text":"grant of development consent, the consent authority must be satisfied that the\nproposed development complies with a number of factors at cl 6.3(2). I note that\nCouncil is satisfied with respect to these technical matters (JS par 53). I accept\nthis advice and am relevantly satisfied.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"18","code":null,"number":"18","title":"Clause 6.7 is concerned with airspace operations. The parties advise that an","text":"appropriate controlled activity approval has been issued by Sydney Airport. I am\nsatisfied with respect to cl 6.7.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"19","code":null,"number":"19","title":"Clause 6.10 concerns design excellence and applies to the proposal. It provides","text":"that consent must not be granted for development to which this clause applies\nunless the consent authority considers that the development exhibits design\nexcellence. Clause 6.10(4) lists a series of matters to have regard to in that\nrespect. The parties advise that this consideration has occurred (JS par 57).\nClause 6.10(5) requires jurisdictional findings of satisfaction. In respect to cl\n6.10(5)(a), the parties advised that a design review panel has reviewed the\ndevelopment. Copies of the relevant report from the panel's meeting of 5\nDecember 2024 were provided to the Court for my own consideration. The parties\nadvise that Council has taken into account these findings in coming to the position\nthat the further amended proposal exhibits design excellence. In turn, with respect\nto cl 6.10(3), Council advises that it considers that the development exhibits\ndesign excellence. I accept this advice and find that I too am satisfied with respect\nto cl 6.10(3).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"20","code":null,"number":"20","title":"","text":"With respect to cl 6.11 and essential services, on the advice of the parties, I am\nsatisfied that water, electricity, sewerage, stormwater and vehicular access\nservices that are essential for the development are available or that adequate\narrangements have been made to make them available when required.\n7 Other provisions of s 4.15(1) of the Environmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"21","code":null,"number":"21","title":"The parties advise that Bayside Development Control Plan 2022 has been taken","text":"into account in assessing the DA, as amended. The parties agree that the\namended plans demonstrate a satisfactory planning outcome when measured\nagainst the relevant controls and objectives.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"22","code":null,"number":"22","title":"The parties have advised that one, in-part, objecting submission was received","text":"when the DA was notified and that it has been given consideration.\nConclusion","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"23","code":null,"number":"23","title":"With the above findings, I am satisfied that the jurisdictional pre-requisites have","text":"been met and the parties' decision is one that the Court could have made in the\nproper exercise of its functions. In turn, I am required under s 34(3) of the LEC\nAct to dispose of the proceedings in accordance with the parties' decision.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"24","code":null,"number":"24","title":"","text":"In making the orders to give effect to the agreement between the parties, I was\nnot required to make, and have not made, any merit assessment of the issues that\nwere originally in dispute between the parties. The LEC Act also requires me to\n\"set out in writing the terms of the decision\" (s 34(3)(b)). The final orders have this\neffect.\nNotations","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"25","code":null,"number":"25","title":"","text":"With respect to the amendments of the DA, the Court notes that:\n(1) Bayside Council, as the relevant consent authority, has approved, under\ns 38(1) of the Environmental Planning and Assessment Regulation 2021\n(NSW), the applicant's further amendment to Development Application DA-\n2024/286 to include the documents set out in Annexure B.\n(2) The Applicant provided the further amended Development Application to\nthe Court on 10 December 2025.\n8 (3) The parties agree that:\n(a) the $40,000 costs referred to in the Court orders (at Order (2) below)\nincludes the costs ordered by the Court in Order 4 of the orders\nmade on 15 October 2025; and\n(b) the $40,000 costs referred to in Order 3(b) below must be paid by\nthe Applicant to the Council in the following manner:\n(i) $10,000 payable on 30 January 2026\n(ii) $10,000 payable on 27 February 2026\n(iii) $10,000 payable on 30 March 2026\n(iv) $10,000 payable on 30 April 2026.\nOrders","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"26","code":null,"number":"26","title":"","text":"The Court orders that:\n(1) The Applicant is granted leave to amend Development Application DA-\n2024/286 to include the documents set out in Annexure B.\n(2) The Applicant is to pay the Respondent's costs thrown away as a result of\nthe amendment of the application, pursuant to s 8.15(3) of the\nEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979 (NSW), in the agreed\namount of $40,000, by no later than 30 April 2026.\n(3) The appeal is upheld.\n(4) Integrated Development Application DA-2024/286, as amended, for the\ndemolition of existing structures and construction of an eleven (11) storey\nshop-top housing development consisting of two (2) commercial tenancies\nat ground floor level with residential co-living above (131 rooms), basement\n9 car parking and landscaping at 465-469 Princes Highway and 5-7 Geeves\nAvenue, Rockdale, is determined by the grant of consent, subject to the\nconditions at Annexure A.\nI certify that this and the preceding 7 pages are a true copy of my reasons for judgment.\nP Walsh\nCommissioner of the Court\n**********\n10 Emag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nAnnexure A\nDETERMINATION OF DEVELOPMENT APPLICATION BY GRANT OF CONSENT\nDevelopment Application No: DA-2024/286\nDevelopment: Integrated Development - Demolition of existing\nstructures and construction of an eleven (11) storey\nshop top housing development consisting of two (2)\ncommercial tenancies at ground floor level with\nresidential co-living above (131 rooms), basement car\nparking and landscaping.\nSite: 465-469 Princes Hwy & 5-7 Geeves Ave, Rockdale\nLot A and Lot B in DP 315664, Lot A in DP 306355, Lot\nA and B in DP 402977 and Lot 1 in DP 131822\nThe above development application has been determined by the granting of consent subject\nto the conditions specified in this consent.\nDate of determination: 19 December 2025\nDate from which consent takes effect: Date of determination\nTERMINOLOGY\nIn this consent:\n(a) Any reference to a Construction, Compliance, Occupation or Subdivision Certificate is\na reference to such a certificate as defined in the Environmental Planning and\nAssessment Act 1979.\n(b) Any reference to the \"applicant\" means a reference to the applicant for development\nconsent or any person who may be carrying out development from time to time\npursuant to this consent.\n(c) Any reference to the \"site\", means the land known as 465-469 Princes Hwy & 5-7\nGeeves Ave, Rockdale.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nThe conditions of consent are as follows:\nThe following conditions restrict the work to the detail provided in the Development\nApplication and are to ensure that the development is complete.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"Approved Plans and Supporting Documentation","text":"Development must be carried out in accordance with the following approved plans and\ndocuments, except where the conditions of this consent expressly require otherwise.\nApproved Architectural Plans\nPlan No. Revision\nNo.\nPlan Title Drawn By Date of\nPlan\n100 H Cover Page Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n100A H Calculations Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n100B H Aerial View Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n100C H Site Analysis Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n100E H Site Photos Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n103 H Survey Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n200 H Basement Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n201 H Ground Floor Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n202 H Level 1 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n203 H Level 2 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n204 H Level 3 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n205 H Level 4 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n206 H Level 5 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n207 H Level 6 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n208 H Level 7-10 Floor Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n209 H Roof Plan Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n300 H East Elevation (Princes\nHwy)\nTier Architects 26/11/2025\n301 H South Elevation\n(Geeves Ave)\nTier Architects 26/11/2025\n302 H West Elevation (Geeves\nLn)\nTier Architects 26/11/2025\n303 H North Elevation Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n304 H Princes Highway\nStreetscape Elevation\nTier Architects 26/11/2025\n400 H Section AA Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n401 H Section BB Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n900 H Materials and Finishes Tier Architects 26/11/2025\n954 D Crane Section Tier Architects 23/10/2025\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nApproved Landscape Plans\nPlan No. Revision Plan Title Drawn By Date of\nNo. Plan\n1 of 5 F Landscape Site Plan Paul Scrivener 25/11/2025\n2698/23 Landscape\n2 of 5 F Level 1 Paul Scrivener 25/11/2025\n2698/23 Landscape\n3 of 5 F Level 3 Paul Scrivener 25/11/2025\n2698/23 Landscape\n4 of 5 F Level 6 Paul Scrivener 25/11/2025\n2698/23 Landscape\n5 of 5 F Calculations Plan Paul Scrivener 25/11/2025\n2698/23 Landscape\nApproved Documents\nDocument Title Version Prepared By Date of Document\nPlan of Management - Emag Apartments November 2025\nAccess Report - Vista Access\nArchitects Pty Ltd\n18/11/2025\nAcoustic Report - West and\nAssociates Pty Ltd\n19/11/2025\nSection J Report - Outsource Ideas Pty\nLtd\n20/11/2025\nWind Impact & Wind\nTunnelling Emulation\nAssessment Report\n- ANA Civil Pty Ltd 19/11/2025\nCombined Demolition /\nConstriction and Operational\nWaste Management Plan\nArcher Consultants\nPty Ltd\nNovember 2025\nIn the event of any inconsistency with the approved plan and a condition of this\nconsent, conditions prevail.\nREASON\nTo ensure all parties are aware of the approved plans and supporting documentation\nthat apply to the development.\n2. Construction Certificate Required\nA Construction Certificate must be obtained from Council or a Principal Certifier prior\nto any building work commencing in relation to staged Construction Certificates.\nBuilding work is defined under the EPA Act Part 6.\nREASON\nTo ensure that a Construction Certificate is obtained at the appropriate time.\n3. Compliance with the Building Code of Australia (BCA)\nBuilding work must be carried out in accordance with the requirements of the BCA.\nREASON\nEP&A Regulation Clause 69(1).\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\n4. Certification of External Wall Cladding\nThe external walls of the building, including attachments must comply with the relevant\nrequirements of the National Construction Code (NCC). Prior to the issue of the\nrelevant Construction Certificate and an Occupation Certificate the Principal Certifier\nmust:\na) Be satisfied that suitable evidence is provided to demonstrate that the products\nand systems proposed for use or used in the construction of external walls\nincluding finishes and claddings such as synthetic or aluminium composite\npanels comply with the relevant requirements of the NCC, and\nb) Ensure that the documentation relied upon in the approval process include an\nappropriate level of detail to demonstrate compliance with the NCC as proposed\nand as built.\nREASON\nTo ensure development complies with the NCC and fire safety requirements.\n5. Amendments Require Modification Application\nFurther alterations and/or additions to the subject building, including the relocation of\nthe fire booster valves and/or provision of an electricity substation, shall not be\nundertaken without first obtaining approval from Council under Section 4.55 of the\nEP&A Act in relation to works requiring development consent.\nREASON\nTo avoid changes that may result in adverse impacts without proper assessment.\n6. Substation / Fire Hydrant Booster\nThe substation and fire hydrant booster are to be located within the footprint of the\nbuilding as per the approved plans. Any change to the location of the aforementioned\nservices or their size will necessitate the submission of a modification application\nunder Section 4.55 of the EP&A Act.\nREASON\nTo ensure minimal impact on the streetscape.\n7. Enclosure of Structures\nCar spaces within the development shall not be enclosed at any future time without\nprior development consent. The outdoor rooftop communal open space area shall not\nbe enclosed at any time in the future without prior development consent.\nREASON\nTo avoid changes that may result in adverse impacts without proper assessment\n8. Earthworks Not Shown on Plans\nNo further excavation, backfilling or retaining walls can be carried out or constructed\nother than those identified on the approved drawings which form part of this consent\nunless it is otherwise permitted as exempt or complying development.\nREASON\nTo avoid changes that may result in adverse impacts without proper assessment.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\n9. Approved Materials and Finishes\nThe finishes, materials and colours scheme approved under condition 1 and any other\nrelevant condition(s) of this consent must not be altered or amended at the\nconstruction certificate stage without a separate Section 4.55 approval.\nREASON\nTo ensure that the development is finished in accordance with the approved plans and\ndocumentation.\n10. Co Living Rooms\nA maximum of 131 co living rooms, being 102 double and 29 single, are permitted on\nsite.\nREASON\nTo quantify the maximum number and breakdown of approved co living rooms.\n11. Parking Allocation\nParking spaces and associated facilities shall be provided and allocated in accordance\nwith the following table.\nUse Number of Car Spaces Allocated\nResidential\nCo Living 16 spaces\nOthers\nLoading Bay 1 SRV\nCar Share 1 space\nMotorcycle parking\nResidential 2 spaces\nNon-Residential 1 space\nBicycle parking 44 spaces\nThe above allocation must be adhered and complied with at all times and shall be\nreflected in any subsequent Strata and/or stratum subdivision of the development.\nAll car share and loading bays shall be stated as common property. All accessible\nparking spaces shall be allocated to adaptable co living rooms.\nREASON\nTo ensure that car parking is provided and allocated in accordance with the approved\nplans and documentation.\n12. Carrying out of works wholly within the Site\nAll approved works shall be carried out inside the confines of the site boundary and not\nin adjacent forecourts, yards, access ways, car parking areas, or on Council's footpath.\nREASON\nTo avoid encroachment of the development beyond the site boundaries.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\n13. Design Architect Involvement\na) In order to ensure the design excellence of the development is retained:\ni) A registered architect is to have direct involvement in the design\ndocumentation, contract documentation and construction stages of the\nproject, and\nii) A registered architect is to have full access to the site and is to be\nauthorised by the applicant to respond directly to the consent authority\nwhere information or clarification is required in the resolution of design\nissues throughout the life of the project, and\niii) Evidence of the Design Architect's commission is to be provided to the\nCouncil prior to release of the first Construction Certificate.\nb) The Design Architect of the project is not to be changed without prior notice and\napproval of the Council.\nREASON\nTo ensure design quality is achieved in accordance with the approved plans and\ndocumentation.\n14. Separate Approval for Use\nA separate application shall be submitted for the use and fit out of the two separate\nground floor commercial tenancies.\nREASON\nTo avoid changes that may result in adverse impacts without proper assessment.\nREQUIREMENTS OF CONCURRENCE, INTEGRATED AND OTHER AUTHORITIES\n15. General Terms of Approval\nThe development shall be carried out in accordance with the requirements of the\nGeneral Terms of Approval (GTA) outlined below.\nApproval Body\nWater NSW\nSydney Trains\nSACL\nTfNSW\nAusgrid\nSydney Water\nDate of GTA\n25 November 2025\n20 August 2025\n16 November 2025\n8 November 2024\n18 November 2024\nA copy of the requirements of the approval Authority is attached to this consent.\nREASON\nTo ensure compliance with General Terms of Approval provided by external\nauthorities.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\n16. Sydney Trains\nThe following conditions are imposed by Sydney Trains/ Transport for NSW in their letter\ndated 20 August 2025 and must be complied with:\n1. TAM land forming Geeves Avenue may be used for vehicular and pedestrian access\nto the proposed development, subject to Bayside Council entering into a licence\nagreement with TAM for the permitted use and ongoing maintenance of the land.\n2. Prior to the issuing of a Construction Certificate, the Applicant shall prepare and\nprovide to Sydney Trains for review, comment, and written endorsement the following\nfinal version rail specific items in compliance with the relevant AMB Standards\n(https://www.transport.nsw.gov.au/industry/asset-management-branch):\n(a)Geotechnical and Structural report/drawings that meet Sydney Trains'\nrequirements. The Geotechnical Report must be based on actual borehole testing\nconducted on the site closest to the rail corridor.\n(b)Construction methodology with construction details pertaining to structural\nsupport during excavation. The Applicant is to be aware that Sydney Trains will\nnot permit any rock anchors/bolts (whether temporary or permanent) within its\nland or easements.\n(c)Detailed Survey Plan showing the relationship of the proposed development with\nrespect to the rail corridor, TAM land and easements. All measurements are to be\nverified by a Registered Surveyor.\n(d)Cross Sectional Drawings showing the rail corridor, sub soil profile, proposed\nbasement excavation and/or structural design of sub ground support adjacent to\nthe rail corridor and TAM Land. All measurements are to be verified by a\nRegistered Surveyor.\nThe Certifier is not to issue the Construction Certificate until written confirmation has\nbeen received from Sydney Trains confirming that this condition has been satisfied.\n3. Given the possible likelihood of objects being dropped or thrown onto the rail\ncorridor from balconies, windows, and other external features (e.g., roof terraces and\nexternal fire escapes) that are within 20 metres of, and face, the rail corridor, the\ndevelopment must have measures installed, to the satisfaction of Sydney Trains (e.g.,\nawning windows, louvres, enclosed balconies, window restrictors etc.) which prevent\nthe throwing of objects onto the rail corridor. The Certifier is not to issue the\nConstruction Certificate until written confirmation has been received from Sydney\nTrains confirming that this condition has been satisfied.\n4. Prior to the issue of a Construction Certificate, the Applicant is to engage an\nElectrolysis Expert to prepare a report on the Electrolysis Risk to the development\nfrom stray currents. The Applicant must incorporate in the development all the\nmeasures recommended in the report to control that risk. A copy of the report is to be\nprovided to the Certifier with the application for a Construction Certificate. The Certifier\nmust ensure that the recommendations of the electrolysis report are incorporated in\nthe construction drawings and documentation prior to the issuing of the relevant\nConstruction Certificate.\n5. Prior to the issuing of a Construction Certificate, the Applicant must submit to\nSydney\nTrains a plan showing all craneage and other aerial operations for the development\nand must comply with all Sydney Trains' requirements. If required by Sydney Trains,\nthe Applicant must amend the plan showing all craneage and other aerial operations to\ncomply with all Sydney Trains' requirements. The Certifier is not to issue the\nConstruction Certificate until written confirmation has been received from the Sydney\nTrains confirming that this condition has been satisfied.\n6. No scaffolding is to be used facing TAM Land unless prior written approval has been\nobtained from Sydney Trains/TAM. To obtain approval the Applicant will be required to\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nsubmit details of the scaffolding, the means of erecting and securing this scaffolding,\nthe material to be used, and the type of screening to be installed to prevent objects\nfalling onto TAM Land.\n7.The Applicant must ensure that all drainage from the development is adequately\ndisposed of and managed and not allowed to be discharged into the rail corridor or\nTAM Land unless prior written approval has been obtained from Sydney Trains/TAM.\n8.During all stages of the development, the Applicant must take extreme care to\nprevent any form of pollution entering the rail corridor and TAM Land. Any form of\npollution that arises as a consequence of the development activities shall remain the\nfull responsibility of the Applicant.\n9.Excess soil is not allowed to enter, be spread, or stockpiled within the rail corridor or\nTAM Land (and its easements) and must be adequately managed/disposed of.\n10.The Applicant/Developer shall not at any stage block any nearby rail corridor\naccess and should make provision for easy and ongoing 24/7 access by rail vehicles,\nplant, and equipment to support maintenance and emergency activities.\n11.The Applicant/Developer shall not at any stage block commuter access to\nRockdale Station commuter carpark and/or Rockdale Railway Station.\n12.If required by Sydney Trains, prior to the issue of a Construction Certificate a Risk\nAssessment/Management Plan and detailed Safe Work Method Statements (SWMS)\nfor the proposed works are to be submitted to Sydney Trains for review and comment\non the impacts on rail corridor. The Certifier is not to issue the Construction Certificate\nuntil written confirmation has been received from Sydney Trains confirming that this\ncondition has been satisfied.\n13.If required by Sydney Trains, prior to the commencement of works or at any time\nduring the excavation and construction period deemed necessary by Sydney Trains, a\njoint inspection of the rail infrastructure and property in the vicinity of the project is to\nbe carried out by representatives from Sydney Trains and the Applicant. These\ndilapidation surveys will establish the extent of any existing damage and enable any\ndeterioration during construction to be observed. The submission of a detailed\ndilapidation report will be required within 10 days following the undertaking of the\ninspection, unless otherwise notified by Sydney Trains.\n14.If required by Sydney Trains, prior to the issue of the Occupation Certificate, or at\nany time during the excavation and construction period deemed necessary by Sydney\nTrains, a joint inspection of the rail infrastructure and property in the vicinity of the\nproject is to be carried out by representatives from Sydney Trains and the Applicant.\nThese dilapidation surveys will establish the extent of any existing damage and enable\nany deterioration during construction to be observed. The Certifier is not to issue the\nfinal Occupation Certificate until written confirmation has been received from Sydney\nTrains confirming that this condition has been satisfied.\n15.Prior to the issue of an Occupation Certificate (whether an interim or final\nOccupation Certificate), a report must be prepared and submitted to the Certifying\nAuthority and Council certifying that the completed development meets the\nrequirements of State Environmental Planning Policy (Transport and Infrastructure)\n2021 and with the Department of Planning and Infrastructure's Development\nAssessment Guideline titled \"Development Near Rail Corridors and Busy Roads -\nInterim Guidelines\" as set down in the subject condition of this consent. Such a report\nmust include external and internal noise levels to ensure that the external noise levels\nduring the test are representative of the typical maximum levels that may occur at this\ndevelopment, and that internal noise levels meet the required dB(A) levels. Where it is\nfound that internal noise levels are greater than the required dB(A) level, necessary\ncorrective measures must be carried out to ensure that internal noise levels are\ncompliant with the requirements of this consent.\n16.Sydney Trains, TAM or Transport for NSW, and persons authorised by those\nentities for the purpose of this condition, must be permitted to inspect the site of the\ndevelopment and all structures to enable it to consider whether those structures have\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nbeen or are being constructed and maintained in accordance with the approved plans\nand the requirements of this consent, on giving reasonable notice to the principal\ncontractor for the development or the owner or occupier of the part of the site to which\naccess is sought.\n17.Copies of any certificates, drawings, approvals/certification, or documents\nendorsed by, given to, or issued by Sydney Trains or TAM (Transport Asset Manager\nof NSW) must be submitted to Council for its records prior to the issuing of the\napplicable Construction Certificate or Occupation Certificate.\n18.The Applicant must ensure that at all times they have a representative (which has\nbeen notified to Sydney Trains in writing), who:\noversees the carrying out of the Applicant's obligations under the conditions of\nthis consent and in accordance with correspondence issued by Sydney Trains;\nacts as the authorised representative of the Applicant; and\nis available (or has a delegate notified in writing to Sydney Trains that is available)\non a 7 day a week basis to liaise with the representative of Sydney Trains, as\nnotified to the Applicant.\n19.Without in any way limiting the operation of any other condition of this consent, the\nApplicant must, during demolition, excavation and construction works, consult in good\nfaith with Sydney Trains in relation to the carrying out of the development works and\nmust respond or provide documentation as soon as practicable to any queries raised\nby Sydney Trains in relation to the works.\n20.Where a condition of consent requires consultation with Sydney Trains, the\nApplicant shall forward all requests and/or documentation to the relevant Sydney\nTrains External Interface Management team. In this instance the relevant interface\nteam is Illawarra Interface, and they can be contacted via email on\nIllawarra_Interface@transport.nsw.gov.au.\n17. Water NSW\nThe following conditions are imposed by WaterNSW in their letter dated 25 November 2025\nand must be complied with:\nDewatering\nGT0115-00001\nGT0119-00001\nGT0120-00001\nGroundwater must only be pumped or extracted for the purpose\nof temporary construction dewatering at the site identified in the\ndevelopment application. For clarity, the purpose for which this\napproval is granted is only for dewatering that is required for the\nconstruction phase of the development and not for any\ndewatering that is required once construction is completed.\nAll extracted groundwater must be discharged from the site in\naccordance with Council requirements for storm water drainage\nor in accordance with any applicable trade waste agreement.\nThe design and construction of the building must prevent: (a)any\ntake of groundwater, following the grant of an occupation\ncertificate (and completion of construction of development), by\nmaking any below-ground levels that may be impacted by any\nwater table fully watertight for the anticipated life of the building.\nWaterproofing of below-ground levels must be sufficiently\nextensive to incorporate adequate provision for unforeseen high\nwater table elevations to prevent potential future inundation;\n(b)obstruction to groundwater flow, by using sufficient permanent\ndrainage beneath and around the outside of the watertight\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nstructure to ensure that any groundwater mounding shall not be\ngreater than 10 % above the pre-development level; and (c)any\nelevated water table from rising to within 1.0 m below the natural\nground surface.\nConstruction Phase Monitoring programme and content: a) A\nmonitoring programme must be submitted, for approval, to\nWaterNSW with the water supply work application. The\nmonitoring programme must, unless agreed otherwise in writing\nby WaterNSW, include matters set out in any Guide published by\nthe NSW Department of Planning Industry and Environment in\nrelation to groundwater investigations and monitoring. Where no\nGuide is current or published, the monitoring programme must\ninclude the following (unless otherwise agreed in writing by\nWaterNSVV): i. Pre-application measurement requirements: The\nresults of groundwater measurements on or around the site, with\na minimum of 3 bore locations, over a minimum period of 3\nmonths in the six months prior to the submission of the approval\nto WaterNSW. ii. Field measurements: Include provision for\ntesting electrical conductivity; temperature; pH; redox potential\nand standing water level of the groundwater; iii. Water quality:\nInclude a programme for water quality testing which includes\ntesting for those analytes as required by WaterNSW; iv. QA:\nInclude details of quality assurance and control v. Lab assurance:\nInclude a requirement for the testing by National Association of\nTesting Authorities accredited laboratories. b) The applicant must\ncomply with the monitoring programme as approved by\nWaterNSW for the duration of the water supply work approval\n(Approved Monitoring Programme)\n(a) Prior to the issuing of the occupation certificate, and following\nthe completion of the dewatering activity, and any monitoring\nrequired under the Approved Monitoring Programme, the\napplicant must submit a completion report to WaterNSW. (b) The\ncompletion report must, unless agreed otherwise in writing by\nWaterNSW, include matters set out in any guideline published by\nthe NSW Department of Planning Industry and Environment in\nrelation to groundwater investigations and monitoring. Where no\nguideline is current or published, the completion report must\ninclude the following (unless otherwise agreed in writing by\nWaterNSW): 1) All results from the Approved Monitoring\nProgramme; and 2) Any other information required on the\nWaterNSW completion report form as updated from time to time\non the WaterNSW website. c) The completion report must be\nsubmitted using \"Completion Report for Dewatering work form\"\nlocated on WaterNSW website www.waternsw.corn.au/customer-\nservice/waterlicensing/dewatering\nThe extraction limit shall be set at a total of 3ML per water year\n(being from 1 July to 30 June). The applicant may apply to\nWaterNSW to increase the extraction limit under this condition.\nAny application to increase the extraction limit must be in writing\nand provide all information required for a hydrogeological\nGT0122-00001\nGT0123-00001\nGT0150-00001\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nassessment. Advisory note: Any application to increase the\nextraction limit should include the following: - Groundwater\ninvestigation report describing the groundwater conditions\nbeneath and around the site and subsurface conceptualisation -\nSurvey plan showing ground surface elevation across the site -\nArchitectural drawings showing basement dimensions -\nEnvironmental site assessment report for any sites containing\ncontaminated soil or groundwater (apart from acid sulphate soils\n(ASS)) - Laboratory test results for soil sampling testing for ASS -\nIf ASS, details of proposed management and treatment of soil\nand groundwater. Testing and management should align with the\nNSW Acid Sulphate Soil Manual\nAny dewatering activity approved under this approval shall cease\nafter a period of two (2) years from the date of this approval,\nunless otherwise agreed in writing by WaterNSW (Term of the\ndewatering approval). Advisory note: an extension of this\napproval may be applied for within 6 months of the expiry of\nTerm.\nThis approval must be surrendered after compliance with all\nconditions of this approval, and prior to the expiry of the Term of\nthe dewatering approval, in condition GT0151-00001. Advisory\nnote: an extension of this approval may be applied for within 6\nmonths of the expiry of Term.\nThe following construction phase monitoring requirements apply\n(Works Approval): a. The monitoring bores must be installed in\naccordance with the number and location shown, as modified by\nthis approval, unless otherwise agreed in writing with WaterNSW.\nb. The applicant must comply with the monitoring programme as\namended by this approval (Approved Monitoring Programme). c.\nThe applicant must submit all results from the Approved\nMonitoring Programme, to WaterNSW, as part of the Completion\nReport\nConstruction phase monitoring bore requirements GTA: a)\nMonitoring bores are required to be installed and collecting data\nprior for at least 3 months prior to submitting a water supply work\napproval b) A minimum of three monitoring bore locations are\nrequired at or around the subject property, unless otherwise\nagreed by WaterNSW. c) The location and number of proposed\nmonitoring bores must be submitted for approval, to WaterNSW\nand should be submitted prior to the\napplication for a water supply work approval. d) The monitoring\nbores should be used to develop a water table map for the site\nand its near environs. e) The monitoring bores must be protected\nfrom construction damage. Advisory note: no approval under the\nWater Management Act 2000 is required for these monitoring\nbores provided that they extract less than 3ML/water year.\nGT0151-00001\nGT0152-00001\nGT0155-00001\nGT0174-00001\nGT0279-00001 A construction certificate can be issued for excavation work in\naccordance with a valid development consent, however\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nGT0281-00001\ndewatering cannot take place without an Approval being granted\nby Water NSW for any water supply works required by the\ndevelopment. If the excavation work will or is likely to require\ndewatering, the applicant must apply and obtain, an approval\nunder the Water Management Act 2000 prior to any dewatering\ntaking place and notify WaterNSW of the programme for the\ndewatering activity including the commencement and proposed\ncompletion dates of the dewatering activity. Advisory Note: An\napproval under the Water Management Act 2000 is required to\nconstruct and/or install the water supply works. For the avoidance\nof doubt, these General Terms of Approval do not represent any\nauthorisation for the take of groundwater, nor do they constitute\nthe grant or the indication of an intention to grant. A water use\napproval may also be required, unless the use of the water is for\na purpose for which a development consent is in force.\nA water access licence, for the relevant water source, must be\nobtained prior to extracting groundwater, unless an exemption\napplies. Advisory Note: See Schedule 4 of the Water\nManagement (General) Regulation 2025.\n18. Sydney Airport Corporation Limited\nThe following conditions are imposed by the Sydney Airport Corporation Limited\n(SACL) in their letter dated 16 November 2025 and must be complied with:\n1. The approved height of 50.90AHD is inclusive of all vents, chimneys, aerials,\nTV antennae and construction cranes etc. No permanent or temporary\nstructure is to exceed this height without further approval from Sydney Airport\nCorporation Limited.\nNote: Under Section 186 of the Airports Act 1996, it is an offence not to give\ninformation to the Airport Operator that is relevant to a proposed \"controlled activity\"\nand is punishable by a fine of up to 50 penalty units.\nFor further information on Height Restrictions please contact SACL on 9667 9246.\nREASON\nTo ensure compliance with requirements of Sydney Airports Corporation Limited.\n19. Ausgrid\nThe following is imposed by Ausgrid and must be complied with:\nAusgrid Underground Cables are in the vicinity of the development.\nSpecial care should be taken to ensure that driveways and any other\nconstruction activities do not interfere with existing underground cables located in the\nfootpath or adjacent roadways.\nIt is recommended that the developer locate and record the depth of all known\nunderground services prior to any excavation in the area. Information regarding the\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nposition of cables along footpaths and roadways can be obtained by contacting Dial\nBefore You Dig (DBYD).\nIn addition to DBYD the proponent should refer to the following documents to support\nsafety in design and construction.\nSafeWork Australia - Excavation Code of Practice.\nAusgrid's Network Standard NS156 which outlines the minimum requirements for\nworking around Ausgrid's underground cables.\nAusgrid cannot guarantee the depth of cables due to possible changes in ground\nlevels from previous activities after the cables were installed.\nShould ground levels change above Ausgrid's underground cables in areas such as\nfootpaths and driveways, Ausgrid must be notified, and written approval provided prior\nto the works commencing.\nShould ground anchors be required in the vicinity of Ausgrid underground cables, the\nanchors must not be installed within 300mm of any cable, and the anchors must not\npass over the top of any cable.\nAusgrid Overhead Powerlines are in the vicinity of the development.\nThe developer should refer to SafeWork NSW Document - Work Near Overhead\nPowerlines: Code of Practice. This document outlines the minimum separation\nrequirements between electrical mains (overhead wires) and structures within the\ndevelopment site throughout the construction process. It is a statutory requirement that\nthese distances be maintained throughout the construction phase.\nConsideration should be given to the positioning and operating of cranes, scaffolding,\nand sufficient clearances from all types of vehicles that are expected be entering and\nleaving the site.\nThe \"as constructed\" minimum clearances to the mains must also be maintained.\nThese distances are outlined in the Ausgrid Network Standard, NS220 Overhead\nDesign Manual. This document can be sourced from Ausgrid's website at\nwww. ausgrid. com. au.\nIt is the responsibility of the developer to verify and maintain minimum clearances\nonsite. In the event where minimum safe clearances are not able to be met due to the\ndesign of the development, the Ausgrid mains may need to be relocated in this\ninstance. Any Ausgrid asset relocation works will be at the developer's cost.\nNew Driveways - Proximity to Existing Poles\nProposed driveways shall be located to maintain a minimum clearance of 1.5m from\nthe nearest face of the pole to any part of the driveway, including the layback, this is to\nallow room for future pole replacements. Ausgrid should be further consulted for any\ndeviation to this distance.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nNew or modified connection\nTo apply to connect or modify a connection for a residential or commercial premises.\nAusgrid recommends the proponent to engage an Accredited Service Provider and\nsubmit a connection application to Ausgrid as soon as practicable. Visit the Ausgrid\nwebsite for further details; https://www.ausgrid.com.au/Connections/Get-connected\nAdditional information can be found in the Ausgrid Quick Reference Guide for Safety\nClearances \"Working Near Ausgrid Assets - Clearances\". This document can be found\nby visiting the following Ausgrid website:\nwww.ausgrid.com.au/Your-safetyNVorking-Safe/Clearance-enquiries\nREASON\nTo ensure the requirements of Ausgrid are adhered to.\n20. Transport for NSW\nThe following conditions are imposed by the Transport for NSW in their letter dated 8\nNovember 2024 and must be complied with:\na. Detailed design plans and hydraulic calculations of any changes to the stormwater\ndrainage system are to be submitted to TfNSW for approval, prior to the\ncommencement of any works. Please send all documentation to\ndevelopment.sydneytransport.nsw.dov.au A plan checking fee will be payable,\nand a performance bond may be required before TfNSW approval is issued.\nb. Any public utility adjustment/relocation works on the state road network will require\ndetailed civil design plans for road opening /underboring to be submitted to\nTfNSW for review and acceptance prior to the commencement of any works. The\ndeveloper must also obtain necessary approvals from the various public utility\nauthorities and/or their agents. Please send all documentation to\ndevelopment sydnetransport.nsw.dov au. A plan checking fee will be payable,\nand a performance bond may be required before TfNSW approval is issued.\nc. The developer is to submit design drawings and documents relating to the\nexcavation of the site and support structures to TfNSW for assessment, in\naccordance with Technical Direction GTD2020/001. The developer is to submit all\ndocumentation at least six (6) weeks prior to commencement of construction and\nis to meet the full cost of the assessment by TfNSW. Please send all\ndocumentation to development sydneytransport.nsw.gov au. If it is necessary\nto excavate below the level of the base of the footings of the adjoining roadways,\nthe person acting on the consent shall ensure that the owner/s of the roadway\nis/are given at least seven (7) day notice of the intention to excavate below the\nbase of the footings. The notice is to include complete details of the work.\nd. The developer is required to enter a Works Authorisation Deed (WAD) with\nTfNSW for the works required by Conditions 1, 2 and 3 that impact the Princes\nHighway.\ne. A Road Occupancy Licence (ROL) should be obtained from the Transport\nManagement Centre (TMC) for any works that may impact on traffic flows on\nBotany Road during construction activities. A ROL can be obtained through\nhttps://myrta.com/oplinc2/paqes/security/oplincLoqin.jsf.\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\nf. The proposed awning along the Princes Highway frontage shall be setback a\nminimum distance of 600mm away from the face of the kerb to protect against\nheavy vehicle overhang.\ng. For the life of the completed development: all servicing of the site (e.g. delivery\ndrop offs and pick ups) for the retail/commercial premises, including waste\ncollection, must be undertaken within the development site and not be undertaken\nfrom the Princes Highway frontage of the site.\nREASON\nTo ensure compliance with requirements of Transport for NSW.\n21. Sydney Water\nThe following conditions are imposed by Sydney Water in their letter dated 18\nNovember 2024 and must be complied with:","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"Further Amended Architectural Plans (Rev H) prepared by TIER","text":"Architects\n- 100: Cover Page\n- 100A: Calculations\n- 100B: Aerial View\n100C: Site Analysis\n100E: Site Photos\n103: Survey Plan\n200: Basement Floor Plan\n201: Ground Floor\n202: Level 1 Floor Plan\n203: Level 2 Floor Plan\n- 204: Level 3 Floor Plan\n205: Level 4 Floor Plan\n206: Level 5 Floor Plan\n207: Level 6 Floor Plan\n208: Level 7-10 Floor Plan\n- 209: Roof Plan\n300: East Elevation (Princes Hwy)\n301: South Elevation (Geeves Ave)\n302: West Elevation (Geeves Ln)\n303: North Elevation\n- 304: Princes Highway Streetscape Elevation\n- 400: Section AA\n- 401: Section BB\n- 502: Area Calculations\n- 503: GFA Calculations\n504: GFA Calculations 2\n505: GFA Calculations 3\n700: Winter Shadows - 9AM\n701: Winter Shadows - 10AM\n702: Winter Shadows - 11AM\n703: Winter Shadows - 12PM\n704: Winter Shadows - 1PM\n705: Winter Shadows - 2PM\n- 706: Winter Shadows - 3PM\n900: Materials and Finishes\n954: Crane Section\n26 November 2025\n2. Further Amended Landscape Plans (Issue F) prepared by Paul\nScrivener:\n25 November 2025\nEmag Apartments Pty Limited v Bayside Council LEC No:2025/94872\n- 1 of 5, Landscape Site Plan\n2 of 5, Level 1 Plan\n3 of 5, Level 3 Plan\n4 of 5, Level 5 Plan\n5 of 5, Calculations Plan\n3. Amended Clause 4.6 - Height - prepared by Planning Ingenuity 19 November 2025\n4. Clause 4.6 - SEPP Car Parking Rates - prepared by Planning\nIngenuity\n19 November 2025\n5 Traffic Statement in Support of Parking Rates prepared by PDC 28 November 2025\n6 Site Hydrogeology Report prepared by Morrow Geotechnics 24 October 2025\n7 Dewatering Management Plan prepared by Morrow Geotechnics 24 October 2025\n8. Remedial Action Plan prepared by El Australia 25 November 2025\n9. Response to Review of Detailed Site Investigation prepared by\nEl Australia 7 November 2025\n10. NABERS Embodied Emissions Materials Form prepared by\nQPC & C 20 November 2025\n11. Private Waste Contractor Confirmation Letter prepared by\nWaste Clear 22 October 2025\n12 (updated) Acoustic Report prepared by West & Associates Pty Ltd 19 November 2025\n13. (updated) Wind Impact 8, Wind Tunnelling Emulation Assessment\nReport prepared by ANA Civil 19 November 2025\n14. (updated) Access Report prepared by Vista Access Architects 18 November 2025\n15. (updated) Section J Report prepared by Outsource Ideas 20 November 2025\n16. (updated) Operational Waste Management Plan prepared by Archer\nConsultants Pty Ltd November 2025\n17. (updated) Plan of Management prepared by Emag Apartments November 2025","stage":"ongoing","stage_basis":"heading","page":94}]});
